ministic output and checking for missing dependencies...\n\nI'm working through the edge direction logic: if A depends on B, B must come before A, so in-degree of a node should equal its number of dependencies, meaning I start the sort from nodes with zero dependencies.\n\nI'm building a reverse adjacency map so I know which nodes depend on each dependency, sorting these lists for deterministic output, then initializing the in-degree count for every node based on its dependency list.\n\nNow I'm implementing a topological sort: starting a queue with nodes that have zero in-degree, repeatedly popping from it, appending to the result, and decrementing the in-degree of dependents—inserting newly-zero nodes back into the queue at the right sorted position to keep ordering deterministic.\n\nI'm pausing to reconsider the approach though, since resorting the whole queue each iteration is wasteful, and I want to think more carefully about guaranteeing a consistent, deterministic ordering across runs for any valid DAG.\n\nStill writing Kahn's algorithm...\n\nProcessing the queue...\n\nThis ensures deterministic ordering by processing nodes lexicographically when ties occur. Now I'm writing the parse package to load JSON node data into a graph, validating that names aren't empty and checking for duplicates before inserting them.\n\nThe plan package should work once graph and parse are fixed—it just loads the graph, orders it, and joins the result into a comma-separated string. Now I'm moving to the cache implementation, which needs to key off the actual input rather than just checking for any cached value, and should avoid caching error results.\n\nTracing through the test cases, I see that after a failed build with a broken input (which isn't cached), a subsequent call with a previously-cached input must still recompute correctly rather than returning stale data. This suggests I need to track the last successful input/result pair specifically, or use a map keyed by input byte