ogical order is \"base,lib,app\" — which matches my logic. I'm also considering the error case where a dependency is missing entirely, which should trigger an error from the graph ordering step.\n\nFor the duplicate name case and the empty name case, both should fail during parsing/loading rather than during ordering. Checking the self-cycle test too: a node with a dependency on itself should have in-degree 1, so it never enters the initial queue and the algorithm should correctly detect this as unresolvable, returning an error rather than accepting it.\n\nFor a missing dependency case like `[{\"Name\":\"x\",\"Deps\":[\"missing\"]}]`, graph.Order should catch that \"missing\" isn't a key in the graph and return an error, confirming that path works. Now I'm satisfied the logic holds, so I'm moving to writing the actual code, checking that the Cache struct's unexported fields behave correctly with zero values under `&Cache{}`, then tracing through the batch test case to verify caching behavior across repeated builds.\n\nI want to keep the style consistent with existing code but fix the bugs. Checking that missing-dependency validation belongs in graph.Order() while parse.Load handles empty names and duplicates — that separation matches the test structure. Tracing through plan test case 1: parse.Load builds the dependency map correctly, then graph.Order sorts names alphabetically and verifies all deps exist in the graph.\n\nFollowing the Kahn's algorithm trace: starting with \"base\" (in-degree 0), processing it reveals \"lib\" becomes ready, then processing \"lib\" makes \"app\" ready, and finally processing \"app\" completes the queue. The result length matches the graph size, confirming correctness, and the final topological order comes out as [\"base\", \"lib\", \"app\"].\n\nDuplicate dependencies within a single node aren't covered by the tests, so I won't handle that edge case specifically. Time to write out the full implementation.\n\n","signature":""}],"contain